{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["Blessed be the name of the exalted El forever, who has done all good for Israel." "And to the engraver He revealed a hidden wonder within the section 'When you take.'" "<b>When you begin</b> to explain the silver of the atonements: it is an obligation upon every individual, and it was not a freewill offering. And he mentioned this because of what is written above, 'once a year he shall make atonement,' and the silver of the atonements is likewise once a year, as I shall explain. And afterward the laver and its base, which are not to be made from the congregation's freewill offering, but only from the mirrors of the serving women alone. Also he mentioned the anointing oil and the incense of spices, for the princes alone brought them. And in mentioning the anointing oil, he mentioned the vessels that are anointed; also he mentioned the great craftsman, who is the compounder and who by whose counsel the entire tabernacle is made, namely Bezalel and Oholiab. And he mentioned the Sabbath to warn them at the outset, lest Israel do the work of HaShem on the Sabbath. And afterward he mentioned the prayer of Moses before his descent from the mountain, and it was necessary to mention first the matter of the calf, to know why he prayed. And afterward he mentioned his descent, and the burning of the calf, and his return to the mountain again to pray a second time on their behalf. And he mentioned the anger of Moses, to remove his tent from the camp of Israel. And some say that the text recounts what happened, and afterward he mentioned the glory of Moses, that he saw the glory, and afterward He commanded him to descend and hew tablets, and HaShem wrote them a second time. And he mentioned the conditions upon which He cut a covenant with Israel — they are those written in the section 'And these are the ordinances.' And afterward he will mention that the skin of his face shone when he descended from the mountain with the tablets of the covenant in his hand. And the Gaon [Saadia] said that the silver of the atonements is an obligation upon Israel every year, whether their king counts them or does not take their number. And he saw it [as the source] from the levy of Moses in the wilderness, and said that from it they offer the congregation's sacrifices and the repair of the laver, and they make anointing oil and incense of spices; also it is given from it to the Sanhedrin, and this is alluded to by Bezalel and Oholiab, for the custom of those who reckon, who are masters of the secret of calculation, when writing in letters, is that the particulars are written at the head of the letter, [and] they write the general [sum] that is compounded from the particulars. Some say that the plague that was in the days of David was because they did not give the ransom of their souls, and if this is true, it is a refutation of the Gaon." "<b>And the sense of 'when you count them.'</b> is that if they do not give their ransom at the moment of counting, then the plague will come. And Yefet said: if they do not give their ransom when they go out to war and are counted, they will be stricken before the enemy."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {2 "Resolved הגאון as 'the Gaon [Saadia]' per the standard epithet-resolution rule. The passage about letters and hidden sums alludes to a cryptographic or numerological convention; translated literally without expanding."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:39:30.330663690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}